Spoiler alert!


There are really a lot of tricks I've figured out.  You add them
together, and you might end up with a good character, if you're lucky.

I have a few of my favorite tricks listed below.  Use them at your own
risk.  Be warned, however, that my character is not a demigod.  He
just has good stats.

There are three ways to get good strength.  Eat spinach.  Eat royal
jelly from a beehive.  Eat giant corpses.  The last one can increase
your strength considerably, while the other two only do by one point.

If you have an identified potion of gain ability, bless it before
using it.  This will help your strength and all your other stats.

The combination of a unicorn horn and a magic marker
makes for excellent armor and weapons.  Here's how:

First, dip the horn into any potion you find.  If it's a blindess,
hallucination, or poison, you'll get a potion of plain water.  Now,
dip some useless scroll into it (many of the scrolls really don't do
you any good), and this will turn them into a scroll of blank paper.
You can do the same thing by dipping them into a fountain.  Now, take
another potion of water (you got by the same or any other method) and
drop it onto a lawful altar.  This gets you instant holy water.  Dip
the scroll in this, and you get a blessed scroll of blank paper.  Use
the magic marker to write "enchant weapon" or "enchant armor" on the
blank scroll.  Now read it while wielding your favorite weapon.  Be
careful not to evaporate it by making it too good!

Some of the monsters on the lower levels tend to rust your weapon when
you hit them.  Black puddings and rust monsters are notorious in this
regard, although acid (and other) blobs will do the same thing.  You
need to rustproof your weapon.  Take a normal (not necessarily
blessed) enchant weapon scroll and read it while confused.  This will
rustproof it.

You can do the same thing to improve your armor.  The trick here is to
only enchant armor that is rustproof already.  Cloaks, gloves,
sometimes boots, and sometimes helmets are rustproof.  Once you get
crystal plate mail or dragon scale mail, you can enchant that.

When you get the wand of wishing, the first things you should wish for
are three blessed scrolls of charging, so you can re-charge the wand.
Be careful about doing this too much, because the dev team took
certain measures to avoid abuse of this....  I'm not sure if the same thing
applies creating scrolls of charging with magic markers, then
re-charging the magic markers to make scrolls of charging....

Get invisibility as quickly as you can.  If you find a wand of
invisibility, zap yourself with it immediately, which is permanent.
If you find a potion of invisibility, bless it (with holy water) and
quaff it, so you can become permanently invisible.  When you're
invisible, you can run away from a fight when you're in trouble.  This
is the only thing that lets people survive, often.

Get speed as soon as you can.  Zap yourself with a want of speed
monster, or eat a quantum mechanic corpse.  Beware eating two,
however.  Speed boots double your speed, and they are arguably the
most valuable items in the game, since you can bop a monster and step
away before he has a chance to hit back.

You get artifacts by sacrificing when your luck is high.  You get high
luck by sacrificing a lot.  One thing to do is to find a level with a
co-aligned altar (or align it yourself) and a kitchen sink.  When you
are of fairly high level, where losing 80 hp's or so won't kill you,
take off all of your rustable armor, and have ready a small, rustable
weapon, and a powerful rustproof weapon.  Now, kick the kitchen sink.
Eventually, you will generate a black pudding.  Hit the pudding with
the small weapon (like an orcish dagger).  It should multiply.  Do
this to each of the children (four puddings), then get out of there.
Puddings lose half of their hp's when they split, so give them a
chance to heal.  Then split them some more.  Now do it again.  You
should be up to your neck in black puddings.  This is fine if you are
invisible and they can't find you.  When you feel you have enough
puddings, start killing them with your rustproof weapon.  Each time
you kill one, sacrifice it or eat it.  Enough eating will eventually
give you cold resistance and shock resistance.  Enough sacrificing
will give you artifacts like crazy.  When you have sacrificed about
ten in a row, you should pray (on the altar, for better results), and
your god should do nice things for you.  Don't pray too often though.  If
you piss off (hi, you Brits) your diety on accident, just keep
sacrificing.  If you're holding a normal longsword in your hand when
you pray, and you aren't chaotic, it may turn into Excalibur, if a
fountain didn't give it to you.

Be warned though.  If you kill 100 puddings and leave the level, your
game will suddenly genocide all those puddings.  In other words, you
only get to do this once.  Also, you don't really need a kitchen sink.
Any level with an altar and a black pudding will do the trick.  Just
remember to bop the puddings with a wimpy, rusted dagger to let them
split without killing them outright.

If you get into a really tight situation with a lot of monsters, the
best spell or scroll is charm monster.  It will get the monsters off
your back and they will start attacking the ones that don't turn tame.
I was struggling with a master lich in the Stronghold when he created
hordes of nasties all over me.  I was about to die when I remembered
this charm monster spell I had saved up.  I fired it off and stopped a
green dragon and a troll from beating up on me.  Lucky, too, because
that was the last time I had that spell memorized.  One of the nasties
was a cockatrice.  I killed that and carefully picked it up with my
gloves.  I waved it in the general direction of the lich until he
petrified.  It was also a good thing I remembered my ring of see
invisible, because that lich ducked out on me.  When I got the wand of
wishing, I wished for three blessed scrolls of genocide and got them.
Guess what the first thing I genocided was?  Yup, all those liches.
They are damned annoying.  That one of them came very close to killing me.
As a further note, a gremlin got in the moat.  If you ever saw the
movie "gremlins" you can guess what happened next.  I had to genocide
them, too, to avoid losing my intrinsics while trying to fight them.

Succubi are very good at getting rid of cursed armor, although it may
cost you money and more....  You can also get succubi by kicking
sinks, not just puddings.

Zap a dead dragon with a wand of polymorph to get really excellent
armor.  If you do this with a gray dragon, you get magic resistance
when you wear it.

Eat wraith corpses as often as possible.  Once you get to 15th level,
it takes a ridiculous amount of experience points to go up any more
levels.  I think 16th level is 320,000 XP's, and each level after that
doubles it, finally maxing out at 10 million XP's per level.  I could
be wrong on this one.

Here's something that's vaguely a cheat.  My current favorite
character is a wizard.  I kept starting with wizards until I got one
with tolerable (13) strength and a magic marker.

Finally, remember:  Nethack is HARD!  You're supposed to lose a lot.
Just keep hacking away.  Remember to run away whenever necessary.
Take your time with the game, and don't rush into bad situations.  You
could get hurt that way.

